  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Why tutorials get this wrong",
      "items": [
        "Postfix reads a restriction list top to bottom; the first permit ends it, so a permit before reject_unauth_destination relays for anyone",
        "Guides copy different lines, often from Postfix versions before 2.10",
        "A setting widened to fix one client can trust the whole internet",
        "Behind Docker or a proxy, every client arrives from the gateway's address"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "What safe looks like",
      "items": [
        "Postfix 2.10+ default: permit_mynetworks, permit_sasl_authenticated, defer_unauth_destination",
        "Postfix 3.0+ trusts only the machine itself (mynetworks_style = host), but only at compatibility_level 2 or higher; upgraded servers often keep the old subnet default",
        "Test from outside: RCPT TO a Gmail address must be refused with 454 or 554",
        "Rate-limit senders: Postfix limits per client IP; per-account limits need a policy service"
      ]
    }
  ]
